.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHDECK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshdeck
.Nd web configurator for nshd
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dv
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
answers the requests of a web page with what
.Xr nshd 8
answers.
.Xr httpd 8
passes it the requests over FastCGI.
The page has seven workspaces.
The overview draws the device's ports as they appear on its front, each
with a link light and an activity light.
It also shows a readout of how many ports are up, the numbers of
interfaces and addresses, and the last change.
The logical interfaces appear in groups by kind, each with what it is on.
Network, routing, security and services each run show commands and draw
their output as tables.
In network, an interface's settings can be changed and staged into the
candidate as lines of configuration.
Routing, security and services list their daemons, with a row for each
routing table a daemon is in.
Each row shows whether the configuration enables the daemon and whether
it runs.
For the chosen daemon, the workspace shows its rules as text, which can
be changed and staged into the candidate as the daemon's edit command in
.Xr nsh 8
does.
Changes not yet staged remain until another daemon or routing table is
chosen.
For a daemon with no rules, it shows the command nsh starts the daemon
with.
In configure, except configure terminal, it shows what the candidate
changes in the daemon.
It also lists each of the last ten saved configurations that changed
the daemon, with what each changed, and shows the daemon's live
operations.
For pf and bgpd, an editor under the table reads the rules' text and
writes changes back into it.
pf's editor shows its statements in order, with a form for each rule.
bgpd's editor shows its peers, its networks and its filters.
For each peer, it shows the peer's state from
.Xr bgpctl 8 ,
the prefixes the peer accepts and advertises, its last errors, the
filters that apply to it, and its other settings in groups.
For ospfd, ospf6d, eigrpd, ripd, ldpd and dvmrpd, an editor shows their
interfaces and their global settings.
For each interface, it shows the settings the interface sets and those
it inherits from the blocks around it.
Beside the editor are the neighbors and routes that their control
programs show in routing table 0.
The system and changes workspace has five topics.
Changes is where configure is entered and lines of configuration are
typed, as
.Xr nsh 8
accepts them, beside a view of the changes, the candidate, the running
configuration or the saved configuration.
History lists the saved configurations, any of which can be shown or
loaded into the candidate.
It also shows nshd's notices and the logs that are followed.
Jobs and MOTD shows root's scheduled jobs and the message of the day, in
the same way as the daemons.
Live operations runs operations, and each is confirmed before it runs.
Errors lists what nshd refused, and the strip shows how many.
The terminal runs
.Xr nsh 8
as the user, and xterm.js draws it.
Tab completes a line as in
.Xr nsh 8 ,
and ? lists what may come next.
The page follows
.Nm nshd Ns 's
notices and logs as they arrive, counts down the time left to confirm
an unconfirmed commit, and draws the interfaces again when they change.
Its strip has Compare, Validate and Commit, which show the changes, run
.Cm commit check ,
and run
.Cm commit .
The strip also shows the generation of the running configuration, when
and by whom it was saved, and the warnings that
.Cm show rollback
prints.
In configure, it also shows how many objects the candidate changes.
While the overview is shown, the page requests the interfaces every 5
seconds.
A port's activity light is on if the port counted packets since the
last request.
When the user logs out, or nshdeck reports that the login has ended,
the page reloads, so that nothing the last user saw remains.
Each user logs in with a name and password, which
.Nm nshd
checks, and has the permissions of the user's login class (see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) .
root may not log in.
.Pp
.Nm
is privilege separated:
.Bl -tag -width "session"
.It parent
Starts as root, binds the socket, and starts the other two.
Then it runs as _nshweb, a member of the group nshadmin, and does one
thing: when session requests a connection, it connects to
.Nm nshd
and passes the connection on.
.Nm nshd
treats a client with _nshweb's uid as the web configurator, which has
no permissions until it logs in.
.It front
Runs as _nshdeck in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It reads the requests
.Xr httpd 8
sends, and checks each complete request before session sees it.
.It session
Runs as _nshweb in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It has a connection to
.Nm nshd
for each user logged in, and knows each user by the token in the user's
cookie.
It writes each answer as JSON.
It completes lines itself, with
.Xr nsh 8 Ns 's
code: it asks
.Nm nshd
for the command tables once, and for the names of interfaces, groups,
bridges and routing tables on each completion.
It asks
.Nm nshd
for each terminal a user opens.
.Nm nshd
starts
.Xr nsh 8
as the user and passes back its pseudo-terminal.
.El
.Pp
If one of them ends,
.Nm
ends.
.Pp
The last part of a request's path names it.
A GET has its fields in its query, and a POST has them in a form body,
of type application/x-www-form-urlencoded.
Each field may be given only once.
Every request except a stream,
.Cm events
or
.Cm terminal ,
must have the header
.Ql X-Nshdeck: 1 ,
which a page of another site cannot send without asking the browser
first.
Every request except
.Cm login
needs the cookie that
.Cm login
sets.
.Bl -tag -width "configure"
.It Cm login
POST, with
.Ar user
and
.Ar password .
Logs the user in, and sets the cookie nshdeck, HttpOnly, Secure and
SameSite=Strict, to a token of 32 random bytes.
The answer has the user's name and permissions.
After 5 failed logins in a row for a name, or from an address, logins
for that name or from that address are refused for a second, without
checking the password.
Each further failure doubles the wait, up to 60 seconds.
The answer's
.Ar wait
is the number of seconds left.
The address is the client's address, as
.Xr httpd 8
gives it in REMOTE_ADDR.
An IPv6 address counts as its /64.
A login that succeeds resets the count for its name, and a count with
no failure for 15 minutes starts again from zero.
.It Cm logout
POST.
Logs the user out.
.Nm nshd
forgets the login and the user's candidate of
.Cm configure private .
.It Cm whoami
GET.
Answers with the user's name and permissions.
.It Cm show
GET, with
.Ar words ,
the words of a show command in
.Xr nsh 8 ,
such as
.Ql interface
or
.Ql route inet 0 .
session sends the show to
.Nm nshd Ns 's
system process.
If the system process does not answer it, session sends it to
.Nm nshd Ns 's
show processes, as
.Xr nsh 8
does.
The answer has the records
.Nm nshd
sent, each an object with its kind and fields.
.It Cm config
GET, with
.Ar what :
running, startup, candidate, diff, or rollback, with or without
.Ar n ;
or changes, with
.Ar n .
The answer is the configuration, as text.
rollback without
.Ar n
lists the saved configurations.
changes is what saved configuration
.Ar n
changed from the one saved before it, as
.Cm show rollback Ar n Cm diff
in
.Xr nsh 8
shows it.
.It Cm configure
POST, with
.Ar mode :
private, shared, exclusive or terminal.
.It Cm line
POST, with
.Ar line :
a line of configuration, in configure.
.It Cm exit
POST.
Leaves configure, or the context a line entered.
.It Cm commit
POST, with
.Ar check
1 to check the candidate only,
.Ar minutes
for
.Cm commit confirmed ,
and a
.Ar comment .
.It Cm rollback
POST, with
.Ar n .
.It Cm op
POST, with
.Ar line ,
an operation such as
.Ql flush routes ,
and its
.Ar rtable .
.It Cm rules
GET, with
.Ar daemon ,
named as
.Xr nsh 8
names it, such as pf or bgp, and its
.Ar rtable .
The answer's
.Ar output
has the daemon's rules in the candidate.
The user must be in configure, but not in
.Cm configure terminal .
.It Cm setrules
POST, with
.Ar daemon ,
.Ar rtable
and
.Ar text ,
up to 1 MB.
Sets the daemon's rules in the candidate, as the daemon's
.Cm edit
in
.Xr nsh 8
does.
With no
.Ar text ,
the rules are removed.
Its body may be up to 3 MB, and at most 4 such bodies may arrive at
once.
.It Cm complete
GET, with
.Ar line ,
and
.Ar help
1 or 0.
Completes the line at its end, in the user's mode, as Tab does in
.Xr nsh 8 .
Each record is of kind insert, with the text to add to the line, or of
kind choice, with a word and its help, if it has one.
With
.Ar help
1, nothing is inserted and every choice is listed.
.It Cm events
GET, with
.Ar logs ,
names joined by commas: daemon, messages, authlog or pflog.
The answer is a stream of events, of type text/event-stream.
The events are
.Nm nshd Ns 's
notices and the lines of each log named, starting from its last lines.
The notices report commits, lines committed in configure terminal,
unconfirmed commits and their rollbacks,
.Cm configure exclusive
entered and left, and changes to interfaces and routes.
authlog needs the admin permission.
A comment is written to the stream every 30 seconds, so that
.Xr httpd 8
does not close it.
If the browser falls more than 1 MB behind, the oldest events not yet
sent to it are dropped, and a dropped event is sent in their place, so
that the page requests again what it shows.
.It Cm terminal
GET, with
.Ar rows
and
.Ar cols ,
each from 1 to 1000; they are 24 and 80 if not given.
Opens a terminal:
.Nm nshd
starts
.Xr nsh 8
on a pseudo-terminal, as the user.
The answer is a stream of events, like that of
.Cm events .
First comes a terminal event, with
.Ar n ,
the terminal's number, from 1 to 4.
Then out events carry what
.Xr nsh 8
writes, in base64, as it arrives.
At the end, an end event has
.Ar text ,
which says why the terminal ended.
The stream is sent only as fast as the browser reads it, and nothing
in it is dropped.
The terminal ends when its stream ends, when
.Xr nsh 8
ends, or when the user logs out.
A user may have at most 4 terminals.
.It Cm type
POST, with
.Ar terminal ,
its number,
.Ar keys ,
what was typed in it, up to 1023 bytes, and
.Ar rows
and
.Ar cols ,
for its new size.
What was typed is written to
.Xr nsh 8
as nsh reads it.
If more than 64 KB would then wait for nsh to read it, the request is
refused with 503.
.El
.Pp
Each answer except an event stream's is a JSON object.
An answer from
.Nm nshd
has
.Ar records ;
.Ar output ,
what
.Nm nshd
printed;
.Ar context ,
where the user is in configure;
.Ar status ;
and
.Ar ok .
It is sent with HTTP status 200, even when
.Nm nshd
refused the request.
Other answers have an
.Ar error
and an HTTP status that says why: 400 for a field that is not accepted,
401 when the user is not logged in or the login failed, 403 for a
request without the header, 404 for an unknown request, 405 for the
wrong method, 413 for a body over 8192 bytes, or over 3 MB for
.Cm setrules ,
415 for a POST that is not a form, 429 for a login while its name or
address must wait, and 503 when
.Nm nshd
does not answer, too many users are logged in, a user has too many
requests waiting or too many terminals, or too many rules are arriving.
.Pp
A user's requests go to
.Nm nshd
one at a time, and at most 16 wait.
A long answer is read from
.Nm nshd
only as fast as the client reads it.
A user with no event stream or terminal open and no request for 30
minutes is logged out.
At most 64 users may be logged in, and
.Nm
accepts at most 128 connections.
It counts the failed logins of at most 512 names and addresses.
It logs a login that fails or is refused at most once every 10 seconds
for its name and address, and each log line says how many logins it did
not log.
Behind a proxy, every client has the proxy's address.
.Pp
.Nm
needs the users _nshweb and _nshdeck, which may not be root or share a
uid, and the group www, which
.Xr httpd 8
runs as.
The socket is owned by root and the group www, with mode 0660.
In
.Xr httpd.conf 5 ,
with the page at
.Pa https://router/nshdeck/ :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
server "router" {
	listen on * tls port 443
	connection max request body 3146752
	location "/nshdeck/api/*" {
		fastcgi socket "/run/nshdeck.sock"
	}
	location "/nshdeck/*" {
		root "/htdocs"
	}
}
.Ed
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl s Ar socket
The socket, instead of
.Pa /var/www/run/nshdeck.sock .
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/www/run/nshdeck.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /var/www/run/nshdeck.sock
the socket
.Xr httpd 8
connects to
.It Pa /var/www/htdocs/nshdeck
the page
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr httpd.conf 5 ,
.Xr httpd 8 ,
.Xr nsh 8 ,
.Xr nshd 8
